# The hero

The hero is the first screen, and it makes its pitch. A meta line sits above a large headline whose last word rotates, and beneath the headline is the fastest way in: the one command that installs the `loom` CLI, given in each runtime's own words, and the command that scaffolds a workspace once the CLI is on. Beside the pitch, a few lines say what Loom is. A reader ready to move can copy a line and start before scrolling any further.

## The rotating headline

The headline is set in three lines, the last of which rotates through a few words — a book, an article, a prompt — fading out and back in on each swap. The view reads the current word from the model's index and the transition phase from its `rotatorPhase`, which drives the class the stylesheet animates.

=>

import { Array, Match } from 'effect'
import type { Html } from 'foldkit/html'
import {
  copyIcon,
  checkIcon,
  bookIcon,
  loomIcon,
  bunIcon,
  denoIcon,
  npmIcon,
  pnpmIcon,
} from './components'
import { Copied, h, type Model } from './model'

export const ROTATOR_WORDS = ['a book', 'an article', 'a spec']

const rotatorClass = (phase: Model['rotatorPhase']): string =>
  Match.value(phase).pipe(
    Match.when('out', () => 'rotator-word out'),
    Match.when('in-start', () => 'rotator-word in-start'),
    Match.orElse(() => 'rotator-word'),
  )

const headline = (model: Model): Html =>
  h.h1(
    [h.Class('loom-h')],
    [
      h.span([h.Class('hl-1')], ['Write your program']),
      h.br([]),
      h.span([h.Class('hl-2')], [`the way you write`]),
      h.br([]),
      h.span(
        [h.Class('rotator-host hl-3'), h.AriaLive('polite')],
        [
          h.span(
            [h.Class(rotatorClass(model.rotatorPhase))],
            [ROTATOR_WORDS[model.rotatorIndex] ?? ROTATOR_WORDS[0]],
          ),
        ],
      ),
      h.span([h.Class('caret'), h.AriaHidden(true)], []),
    ],
  )

## The install rows

Under the headline the install runs as a short stack of rows — one per runtime, in the order a reader is likely to reach for: bun, deno, npm, pnpm. Each row leads with the runtime's coloured mark and shows the one command that installs the `loom` CLI from it. The whole row is the button: a click copies the command, a copy icon fades in on hover to invite it, and the pressed row answers with a mint check. The command names the runtime and the mark carries its colour, so the two are label enough. The CLI installs the same way from any of them, so only the command's words change from row to row.

Yarn is left out — its global install works only on Yarn 1, so a command shown here would fail on the version most readers now run.

=>

type Runtime = {
  readonly id: string
  readonly command: string
  readonly icon: Html
}

const RUNTIMES: ReadonlyArray<Runtime> = [
  { id: 'bun', command: 'bun add -g @athrio/loom', icon: bunIcon() },
  { id: 'deno', command: 'deno install -g -n loom npm:@athrio/loom', icon: denoIcon() },
  { id: 'npm', command: 'npm install -g @athrio/loom', icon: npmIcon() },
  { id: 'pnpm', command: 'pnpm add -g @athrio/loom', icon: pnpmIcon() },
]

const rowCopy = (id: string, copied: string): Html => {
  const done = copied === id
  return h.span(
    [h.Class(done ? 'rt-copy copied' : 'rt-copy')],
    [done ? checkIcon() : copyIcon()],
  )
}

const installRow = (copied: string) => (runtime: Runtime): Html =>
  h.button(
    [
      h.Class('install-row'),
      h.AriaLabel(`Copy: ${runtime.command}`),
      h.OnClick(Copied({ id: `install-${runtime.id}`, text: runtime.command })),
    ],
    [
      h.span([h.Class('rt-mark')], [runtime.icon]),
      h.code([h.Class('rt-cmd')], [runtime.command]),
      rowCopy(`install-${runtime.id}`, copied),
    ],
  )

const installRows = (model: Model): Html =>
  h.div([h.Class('install-rows')], Array.map(RUNTIMES, installRow(model.copied)))

## What Loom is

Beside the pitch, the right column says what Loom is. A lead line names it, then four points — each marked with the loom itself — say what it does, and a closing line hands the rest to prose and code in any language. A point that names Loom's syntax primitives wears each one as a small monospace chip; the anchor chip is built from pieces so no bare anchor sits in this corpus.

=>

const prim = (token: string): Html =>
  h.code([h.Class('hero-prim')], [token])

const point = (content: ReadonlyArray<Html | string>): Html =>
  h.li([], [h.span([h.Class('hero-bullet')], [loomIcon()]), h.span([], content)])

const whatLoom = (): Html =>
  h.div(
    [h.Class('hero-note')],
    [
      h.p(
        [h.Class('hero-lead')],
        ['Loom is a compositional language designed for AI-assisted literate programming.'],
      ),
      h.ul(
        [h.Class('hero-points')],
        [
          point(['Provides the tools to write programs in the order demanded by natural language, logic, and the flow of thought.']),
          point(['Keeps specification alongside code, while structurally separating the two.']),
          point([
            'Introduces a minimal syntax surface over standard Markdown, with intuitive primitives like ',
            prim('=>'),
            ', ',
            prim('~'),
            ', ',
            prim('::' + '[]'),
            ', and ',
            prim('{}'),
            '.',
          ]),
          point(['Installs as a CLI with integrated LSP and MCP devtools.']),
        ],
      ),
      h.p(
        [],
        ['Everything else is just prose and code in your favourite programming language.'],
      ),
    ],
  )

## The hero column

The hero opens with the meta line — a pill naming the latest release, which the server fills from npm so it never falls behind — then lays out in two columns. On the left is the pitch: the rotating headline, the install rows beneath it, and the two calls to action — read the docs, browse the source. On the right, a few lines say what Loom is. The two columns start at the same top, so the statement lines up with the headline. On a narrow screen they fall into one, the statement below the pitch.

=>

const metaRow = (version: string): Html =>
  h.div(
    [h.Class('meta-row')],
    [
      h.span(
        [h.Class('pill')],
        [h.span([h.Class('pill-mark')], [loomIcon()]), `loom · v${version}`],
      ),
    ],
  )

const cta = (): Html =>
  h.div(
    [h.Class('actions hero-cta')],
    [
      h.a([h.Class('btn primary'), h.Href('#')], ['Read the docs', bookIcon()]),
      h.a([h.Class('btn'), h.Href('#')], ['Browse the source']),
    ],
  )

const pitch = (model: Model): Html =>
  h.div([h.Class('hero-col')], [headline(model), installRows(model), cta()])

export const hero = (model: Model): Html =>
  h.section(
    [h.Class('hero')],
    [
      h.div(
        [h.Class('wrap')],
        [
          metaRow(model.version),
          h.div([h.Class('hero-grid')], [pitch(model), whatLoom()]),
        ],
      ),
    ],
  )

## The module {Tangle}[hero.ts]

The module is the rotating headline, the install rows beneath it, the lines that say what Loom is, and the section that lays the pitch — headline, install rows, and calls to action — on the left and that statement on the right.

